# Online Support Bubble (OSB)

A runnable, terminal-based proof-of-concept implementation of the privacy-preserving
support-group protocol described in:

> N. Desai, C. C. Drăgan, A. Fraser, N. Sastry, S. Schneider, E. Toreini, H. Treharne,
> and K. I. Turk, **"Online Support Bubble: a Privacy Preserving Support Group Mechanism."**
> (`AP4L_Support_Bubble_2026.pdf`, included in the parent directory.)

An *online support bubble* lets a **subject** (a person seeking sensitive help) assemble an
anonymous group of **advisors** chosen by their *attributes* — e.g. `cancer_specialist`,
`legal_advisor`, `experience_gt_5y` — ask questions, and receive auditable answers, while the
system guarantees:

* **Unlinkability** — nobody can link a user across different support bubbles, nor tell whether
  a given person is in any bubble at all.
* **Non-frameability** — nobody (not even the moderators) can impersonate an honest user or frame
  them for messages they did not send.
* **Accountable tracing** — a misbehaving member *can* be de-anonymised, but only when two
  independent authorities (the **Registrar** and the **Tracer**) cooperate.

> ⚠️ **This is a research proof-of-concept, not production software.** The cryptography is
> implemented in pure Python for clarity and is **not** constant-time, side-channel resistant, or
> audited. Do not use it to protect real users.

---

## Scope decisions

The paper assumes a distributed ledger (blockchain) and proves security in that setting. This
implementation makes two deliberate simplifications, agreed for the project:

| Paper | This implementation |
|-------|---------------------|
| Distributed ledger / blockchain | A single **centralized server** behind a `LedgerPort` interface |
| (No UI specified) | A **terminal CLI** only (no GUI) |

Because the ledger is accessed through a port, swapping the centralized server for a real
distributed ledger later would not touch any protocol or cryptographic code.

---

## Architecture

The code follows a **hexagonal (ports-and-adapters) architecture** around a **pure cryptographic
core**. Dependencies always point inward: `cli → adapters → entities → protocol → crypto`.

```
osb/
├── osb/                         the package
│   ├── crypto/                  L1  pure cryptography (no I/O, no protocol knowledge)
│   │   ├── primitives/          bilinear pairing wrapper, Schnorr signatures, AEAD
│   │   ├── as_scheme/           Asynchronous Remote Key Generation (ARKG)
│   │   ├── gs_scheme/           Group Signature with Verifier-Local Revocation
│   │   └── be_scheme/           Public-key Broadcast Encryption
│   ├── protocol/                L2  pure domain logic
│   │   ├── models/              dataclasses (keys, tokens, tuples, ledger entries)
│   │   ├── policy/              CNF support policy Ψ over attributes
│   │   └── algorithms/          Register + the support-bubble algorithms (paper Figs 2-3)
│   ├── entities/                L3  the actors: registrar, tracer, ledger, subject, advisor
│   ├── ports/                   L4  interfaces: StoragePort, LedgerPort, TransportPort
│   ├── adapters/                L4  storage (in-memory), server (central), transport (in-proc)
│   ├── cli/                     L5  argparse entrypoint, interactive shell, scripted demo
│   ├── config/                  curve parameters, attribute catalogue, settings
│   └── profiling.py             per-function time_log / space_log instrumentation
├── tests/                       crypto / protocol / entities / e2e test suites
├── scripts/                     benchmark.py
└── pyproject.toml
```

### Cryptographic building blocks

All schemes run over the **BLS12-381** pairing-friendly curve via the
[`py_ecc`](https://github.com/ethereum/py_ecc) library (`optimized_bls12_381`). The library is
isolated behind `crypto/primitives/pairing.py`, the only file that imports it.

| Scheme | File | Construction | Hard problem |
|--------|------|--------------|--------------|
| **AS** — Asynchronous Remote Key Generation | `crypto/as_scheme` | Discrete-log ARKG in G1: `dpk = pk·g^ck`, `ck = H(DH-secret)`, credential authenticated with HMAC | CDH / GDH in G1 |
| **GS** — Group Signature w/ Verifier-Local Revocation | `crypto/gs_scheme` | SDH membership credential `A = g1^{1/(γ+x)}` + Fiat–Shamir NIZK over a re-randomised `Â = A^t`; revocation tag `(Â, η^t)` with `η = hash_to_g2(m‖nonce)` | q-SDH in G1, ROM |
| **BE** — Public-key Broadcast Encryption | `crypto/be_scheme` | Boneh–Gentry–Waters "basic" scheme, broadcasting to the full member set; type-III adaptation | q-BDHE |
| **Schnorr** signature | `crypto/primitives/schnorr.py` | Key-prefixed Schnorr over G1 (used by AS for signing) | DLog in G1, ROM |
| **AEAD** | `crypto/primitives/aead.py` | Encrypt-then-MAC (SHA-256 keystream + HMAC-SHA-256), stdlib only, for the symmetric layer of BE | — |

`hash_to_g2` uses the RFC 9380 (`hash-to-curve`) implementation shipped with `py_ecc`; an
unknown-discrete-log base is what gives the group signature its *selfless-anonymity*.

### Protocol entities

| Entity | Responsibility |
|--------|----------------|
| **Registrar** | Onboards users, verifies their registration (`GS.Vf ∧ AS.Vf`), keeps the private identity map `uL` and the public permitted-key list `PKL`. Runs `Judge` during tracing. |
| **Tracer** | Holds the group-signature manager key `tsk`, issues group keys at registration, maintains the public revocation list `cpkL`, runs `Trace`. |
| **Ledger** | The append-only public store + authorisation-token accounting (`Register`, `Add`, `Get`, `Update`, `Search`). Tokens bind a location to its creator so only the creator can later update/close it. |
| **Subject / Advisor** | Client-side actors (`entities/subject`, `entities/advisor`) — facades over the protocol algorithms. A subject builds and moderates bubbles; advisors join by attribute. |

Tracing is deliberately split: the **Tracer** recovers a revocation token from a signature, and only
the **Registrar** can map that token back to a real identity. Neither can de-anonymise a user alone.

---

## Installation

Requires **Python ≥ 3.10**. From the `osb/` directory:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -e ".[dev]"     # installs py_ecc and pytest
```

This also installs an `osb` console command. The examples below use `python -m osb`, which works
without activating the venv.

---

## Usage

### Quick info

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m osb info
```

### End-to-end demo (scripted)

Runs a full scenario — register a subject and advisors, form a bubble by policy, advisors join,
exchange sign-then-encrypt messages, replace an advisor, trace a member, close the bubble:

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m osb demo
```

> Real BLS12-381 pairings in pure Python are slow; the demo takes roughly **30–60 seconds**.

### Interactive shell

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m osb shell
```

Then drive the protocol yourself. A policy is written as `clause;clause`, where each clause is a
comma-separated list of attributes (the policy is satisfied when **every** clause has at least one
matching advisor — conjunctive normal form):

```
osb> register_subject alice
osb> register_advisor dr_oncology cancer_specialist,experience_gt_5y
osb> register_advisor ms_law legal_advisor
osb> advisors
osb> bubble alice cancer_specialist;legal_advisor
osb> join b0 dr_oncology
osb> join b0 ms_law
osb> send b0 alice I was just diagnosed, what are my options?
osb> inbox b0 dr_oncology
osb> trace b0 ms_law
osb> close b0
osb> status
osb> quit
```

Type `help` inside the shell for the full command list. Available attributes are shown by
`osb info`.

---

## `time_log` and `space_log`

Every `demo` or `shell` run is profiled, and on completion two files are written **to the current
working directory**, each **overwritten (reset) on every run**:

* **`time_log`** — wall-clock time per instrumented function, in seconds, sorted by cumulative time.
  `cumulative_s` includes callees; `self_s` is the function's own time.
* **`space_log`** — memory attributed per instrumented function, sorted by cumulative usage.

Instrumentation covers every OSB operation (registration, the bubble-lifecycle algorithms,
messaging, trace/judge) and every cryptographic primitive call. The profiler is a lightweight
`perf_counter` / `getrusage` decorator, so it adds **negligible overhead** (a profiled demo runs in
about the same time as an unprofiled one).

**Space metric.** By default `space_log` reports the rise in **peak resident-set size**
(`getrusage`) attributable to each function. This is cheap but coarse: because peak RSS only ever
grows, functions that run after the peak is reached show `0`. For detailed **net bytes allocated per
function** set the environment variable (this uses `tracemalloc` and is ~6× slower):

```bash
OSB_PROFILE_MEM=tracemalloc .venv/bin/python -m osb demo
```

Example `time_log` excerpt:

```
  cumulative_s      self_s   calls  function
----------------------------------------------------------------
     17.384742   17.384742      11  crypto.gs_scheme.verify
      7.505458    7.505458       9  crypto.gs_scheme.sign
     11.984750    0.003510       5  protocol.algorithms.registration.register_user
```

---

## Testing

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m pytest
```

The suite (105 tests) covers the cryptographic primitives (correctness, unforgeability,
unlinkability, revocation, broadcast round-trips), the protocol layer (policy, ledger, registrar,
tracer), and end-to-end flows (registration, bubble formation, messaging, moderation, tracing) plus
the security-property checks in `tests/e2e/test_security.py`. It takes ~90 s because of the
pure-Python pairings.

## Benchmarking

Reproduces the per-operation timing study of the paper (its Figure 4):

```bash
.venv/bin/python scripts/benchmark.py
```

---

## Paper → code map

| Paper | Code |
|-------|------|
| Register protocol (Fig. 2) | `protocol/algorithms/registration.py::register_user` |
| `getAdvisorsS` (Fig. 3) | `protocol/algorithms/bubble.py::get_advisors` |
| `startGroupS` | `bubble.py::start_group` |
| `joinGroupA` | `bubble.py::join_group` |
| `closeGroupS` | `bubble.py::close_group` |
| `removeAdvisorS` / `replaceAdvisorS` | `bubble.py::remove_advisor` / `replace_advisor` |
| Group communication (Sec. IV-B) | `protocol/algorithms/messaging.py` |
| `Trace` / `Judge` | `adapters/server/server.py::osb_trace` / `entities/registrar::judge` |
| Public ledger `PL` (Sec. II-F) | `entities/ledger`, `ports/ledger.py` |

---

## Limitations

* Pure-Python, non-constant-time cryptography — for correctness demonstration only.
* The centralized server holds all in-memory state; there is no persistence or network transport
  (the in-process transport is one `TransportPort` adapter; an HTTP adapter would be another).
* `getAdvisorsS` uses a greedy set-cover over registered advisors rather than enumerating every
  satisfying advisor set.
* In the in-process simulation the CLI session holds every member's secret keys so it can act on
  their behalf; a real deployment would distribute those keys to the respective users.

